      "citation": "Shi, B., Catsamas, S., Deletic, A., et al. (2022). Illicit discharge detection in stormwater drains using an Arduino-based low-cost sensor network. Water Science and Technology, 85(5), 1372–1383. https://doi.org/10.2166/wst.2022.034",
      "intro": "Shi et al. detail the design and deployment of an open-source, low-cost sensor network measuring electrical conductivity and temperature to detect illicit domestic sewage cross-connections in stormwater drains.",
      "methods": "Built Arduino-based sensor nodes equipped with toroidal/conductive electrical conductivity (EC) probes and DS18B20 temperature sensors, deployed across an urban stormwater catchment in Melbourne, Australia.",
      "scope": "Stormwater drains, dry-weather baseflow, sewage cross-connection detection, and low-cost hardware fabrication (<$300 USD per node).",
      "usefulness": "Demonstrates how electrical conductivity and diurnal temperature fluctuations serve as reliable, low-cost proxies for illicit wastewater ingress and seawater infiltration.",
      "limitations": "Biofilm growth and mineral scaling on direct-contact galvanic electrodes caused signal drift over 3–4 weeks, highlighting the superiority of toroidal inductive probes.",
      "conclusions": "High-frequency EC and temperature time series accurately flagged illicit greywater and blackwater discharges by detecting conductivity spikes during dry weather.",
      "reflection": "Cited in Section 5 of Paper 1 to support the analysis of electrical conductivity sensing for tracking water quality anomalies and rainwater dilution."
